# Two mechanisms in the best-train law (#10) both decide WHICH frontier to walk to: the unknown-space weighting (#3, +5 over confinement) and the sweep away from the blurred past path. If the sweep already sends the agent toward big unexplored ground (what lies away from where it has been is mostly unknown), the unknown weighting is redundant there. This ablation removes `unknown`/`vast` from #10 as tuned: if it eats about the same, the sweep subsumes the information-gain weight; if it drops ~5, they are separate effects.

channel source
channel solid
channel free
channel thing
channel scent
channel here
channel value
channel confinement
channel dwell
channel frust
channel trail
channel been
channel injection

rule confine:
  when 1
  set confinement = sum8(solid)

rule dwell:
  when 1
  set dwell = where(here > 0, min(1, dwell + 0.005203), 0.7418 * dwell)

rule frust:
  when 1
  set frust = max(here * dwell, 0.95 * max8(frust))

# the path walked, slowly fading; scrolls with the map
rule trail:
  when 1
  set trail = max(here, 0.998 * trail)

# a wide blur of the path: how much of the agent's own past lies around a cell
rule been:
  when 1
  set been = 0.2 * trail + 0.8 * avg8(been)

# frontier on the side the agent came from counts less: the sweep goes on away from its past
# ablation: every frontier mark weighs the same again; only the sweep (been) shapes exploration
rule inject:
  when 1
  set injection = max(max(thing, source * where(thing + scent > 0, 1.358, (1 - min(1, 3 * been)))) / (1 + 0.4454 * confinement), frust * solid * max8(free) * leak)

rule wave:
  when 1
  set value = max(injection, max(0.94 * max4(value), 0.916214 * max8(value)) * (0.85 + 0.15 * min(1, free))) * (1 - solid * (1.028 - leak))
